00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:07.040
And it's structures these
in a really intuitive way.

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:10.000
So for me, building
vision-based AutoML models,

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:13.660
it's a really nice new sort of
unified UI for the structure

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:17.320
of annotations to the raw data.

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:19.370
KARL WEINMEISTER: Yeah,
that's a great point.

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:21.410
What I think is really
awesome about Vertex

00:03:21.410 --> 00:03:23.690
is that it sort
of brings together

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:28.010
a lot of AI technologies into
one place like Zack mentioned.

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:31.900
This idea of creating what
are called Managed Data Sets.

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:33.860
So it's the same
data that you can

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:36.710
use if you're going to create
different types of vision

00:03:36.710 --> 00:03:38.870
models, let's say
for classification,

00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:41.057
object detection,
whatever you're doing.

00:03:41.057 --> 00:03:42.890
But also, if you're
building a custom model,

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:46.070
so you're using TensorFlow
and writing a model

00:03:46.070 --> 00:03:49.130
yourself with code, you
have that traceability back

00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:51.500
to that same data set.

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:54.950
And Vertex brings all these
different services together.

00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:56.960
So it's like you load your data.

00:03:56.960 --> 00:03:58.040
You build a model.

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:01.910
Then the next thing is deploying
that model to an endpoint.

00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:04.130
It's all-- kind of walks
you through a flow,

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:06.920
so it's really intuitive
and brings it all together

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:10.610
and allows you different
types of roles, whether you're

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:12.710
a developer or data
scientist, to be

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:15.650
able to use the tool easily.

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:19.050
So that's Vertex.

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:23.360
Let's go ahead and move on
to our next question here.

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:26.840
It is about AI solutions.

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:31.250
So we talked about how
Vertex brings a lot

00:04:31.250 --> 00:04:33.895
of different services together.

00:04:33.895 --> 00:04:35.270
Another way to
think about things

00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:37.400
is different levels
of abstraction, right?

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:40.430
So if you're building
an AI solution,

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:42.650
the question is, what are
those different levels

00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:43.520
of abstraction?

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:46.050
And I can start with this one.

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:48.650
So we talked about
custom models, right?

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:50.240
That's the ability
that you typically

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:52.070
think of with data
science, where

00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:55.940
you might visualize your
data, write some code

00:04:55.940 --> 00:05:00.800
to train the data, build
a model, take that file,

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:03.280
deploy it to an endpoint, and
then predict with it, right?

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:04.980
So that's that sort of process.

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:07.640
Now, AutoML has come
along and allowed

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:10.400
you to create custom models
with your unique data

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:14.000
to solve unique problems, but
without having to figure out

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:15.660
the best model for it.

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:17.730
So that's another
level of abstraction.

00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:20.490
It kind of takes some of
those steps of the process

00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:21.750
and does it for you.

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:25.700
And then you also have APIs
that allow you to not even bring

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:28.670
your own data, simply leverage
what Google has on the cloud.

00:05:28.670 --> 00:05:32.520
Like, let's provide an image
and get some insights out of it.

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:34.370
What's in that image?

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:35.708
Or what are some of the--

00:05:35.708 --> 00:05:38.000
let's put some bounding boxes
around some of the things

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:39.480
that we find in that image.

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:41.105
These are all things
that we've trained

00:05:41.105 --> 00:05:43.880
against a large general
data set so we can apply it

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:45.600
to all kinds of problems.

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:48.110
Finally, one of
the exciting things

00:05:48.110 --> 00:05:52.400
that Anu mentioned that she
focuses on is AI solutions.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:55.160
And here's where we're building
business-oriented solutions

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:57.800
for things like
managing documents,

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:00.030
parsing forms, all
those kind of things.

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:04.190
We've taken the
AI brain behind it

00:06:04.190 --> 00:06:05.840
and made it very easy to use.

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:08.700
So you can work at all
these different levels.

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:12.470
So as kind of an intro to
it, Zack, anything else

00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:14.390
you want to add about
the different ways

00:06:14.390 --> 00:06:17.217
you can work with
Google Cloud AI?

00:06:17.217 --> 00:06:18.800
ZACK AKIL: Well,
yeah, you pretty much

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:22.070
covered it all in terms of
like, when I'm building stuff,

00:06:22.070 --> 00:06:25.770
I sort of come at it from
a developer standpoint.

00:06:25.770 --> 00:06:27.790
So I look at what's the
simplest and quickest

00:06:27.790 --> 00:06:29.040
way for me to solve a problem.

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:31.550
So naturally, I'll
go to the APIs first.

00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:34.700
OK, if I can solve a problem
using the APIs, that's perfect.

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:36.150
I can finish there.

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:38.750
But then if the APIs don't
quite do exactly what I want,

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:40.880
I build a custom
model with AutoML.

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:44.210
And then if AutoML's patterns
don't match what I'm doing,

00:06:44.210 --> 00:06:48.085
I do a custom model with
custom training routines

00:06:48.085 --> 00:06:48.960
and things like that.

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:52.970
So yeah, pretty much the
exact same thing you said.

00:06:52.970 --> 00:06:55.640
ANU SRIVASTAVA: Yeah, it's
