00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:19.480
OK, so our next question,
let's talk about MLOps.

00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:23.320
OK, so the question
is, "It seems

00:11:23.320 --> 00:11:27.190
that Vertex AI has a lot
of 'MLOps' capabilities.

00:11:27.190 --> 00:11:30.790
Why is MLOps important
to developers?"

00:11:30.790 --> 00:11:33.080
And I can start with this one.

00:11:33.080 --> 00:11:36.610
So in the development
world, we've

00:11:36.610 --> 00:11:38.230
had some practices
for a long time,

00:11:38.230 --> 00:11:41.080
right-- continuous integration,
continuous development.

00:11:41.080 --> 00:11:43.120
We have ways of
unit tests that we

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:47.200
can check that what we're
doing is going to work,

00:11:47.200 --> 00:11:50.230
that as we make changes to our
code, it's not going to break.

00:11:50.230 --> 00:11:53.920
Now machine learning is making
mission-critical decisions

00:11:53.920 --> 00:11:54.760
about things.

00:11:54.760 --> 00:11:57.760
It's classifying things,
predicting things.

00:11:57.760 --> 00:12:01.630
We need that same level
of rigor around the models

00:12:01.630 --> 00:12:04.450
that we build to prevent
a disaster from happening,

00:12:04.450 --> 00:12:06.200
a bad model going
into production.

00:12:06.200 --> 00:12:07.330
So MLOps is all about it.

00:12:07.330 --> 00:12:10.135
So Vertex AI has some really
interesting capabilities

00:12:10.135 --> 00:12:10.880
built in.

00:12:10.880 --> 00:12:13.060
So one is called
Manage Pipelines.

00:12:13.060 --> 00:12:15.190
And Manage Pipelines
allows you to create

00:12:15.190 --> 00:12:18.100
a reproducible pipeline,
a set of steps,

00:12:18.100 --> 00:12:20.530
where you can basically
hit the Play button.

00:12:20.530 --> 00:12:23.650
And it will, if you
code it this way,

00:12:23.650 --> 00:12:26.590
extract data from your data
sources, build a model,

00:12:26.590 --> 00:12:30.250
deploy your model,
maybe validate the data

00:12:30.250 --> 00:12:32.290
that you're using to
make sure that some

00:12:32.290 --> 00:12:34.123
of the data that you're
pulling from-- there

00:12:34.123 --> 00:12:36.130
weren't changes into
the schema or anything.

00:12:36.130 --> 00:12:38.110
And then maybe you
evaluate your model

00:12:38.110 --> 00:12:40.780
and make sure that the
accuracy hasn't gone down

00:12:40.780 --> 00:12:42.230
before you deploy it.

00:12:42.230 --> 00:12:45.410
Those are all the kind of things
that you can do in a pipeline.

00:12:45.410 --> 00:12:48.850
Another really cool thing
is called Model Monitoring.

00:12:48.850 --> 00:12:51.670
And I know Zack wrote
an article about this

00:12:51.670 --> 00:12:54.790
a while back about zombie
data in your models.

00:12:54.790 --> 00:12:56.510
It was very cool,
good introduction

00:12:56.510 --> 00:12:58.420
that you could find on Medium.

00:12:58.420 --> 00:13:02.050
And it's this idea
that you build models

00:13:02.050 --> 00:13:03.700
based on data in the past.

00:13:03.700 --> 00:13:05.350
But we all know
things change, right?

00:13:05.350 --> 00:13:08.200
And so that model that
had really good accuracy

00:13:08.200 --> 00:13:10.120
could start to drift over time.

00:13:10.120 --> 00:13:12.280
And that's where Model
Monitoring comes in.

00:13:12.280 --> 00:13:14.740
It's not like typical
software development

00:13:14.740 --> 00:13:16.420
where it just breaks--

00:13:16.420 --> 00:13:18.580
it's works, or it doesn't.

00:13:18.580 --> 00:13:21.010
ML, there's a little more
nuance, where it could work,

00:13:21.010 --> 00:13:22.900
but it just starts
to degrade over time.

00:13:22.900 --> 00:13:25.190
And Model Monitoring
[? allow you ?] to catch that.

00:13:25.190 --> 00:13:29.900
So these are just a couple of
examples of the integrated ways

00:13:29.900 --> 00:13:32.480
that you can apply
DevOps principles to ML

00:13:32.480 --> 00:13:34.170
that are all part of Vertex AI.

00:13:34.170 --> 00:13:38.090
So definitely recommend
checking that out.

00:13:38.090 --> 00:13:42.620
OK, so our next question
is about leveraging

