00:19:55.430 --> 00:19:58.750
So our next question, I
think, is a really good one

00:19:58.750 --> 00:20:01.660
from the audience, especially
if you're a developer,

00:20:01.660 --> 00:20:03.670
and you're not
really sure, should I

00:20:03.670 --> 00:20:05.220
apply AI to this or not?

00:20:05.220 --> 00:20:08.680
So the question is, "How do
you spot a problem which can be

00:20:08.680 --> 00:20:09.395
'AI'ified'"--

00:20:09.395 --> 00:20:13.030
I like that-- "versus more
conventional methods?"

00:20:13.030 --> 00:20:15.760
And I can start
off with this one,

00:20:15.760 --> 00:20:19.150
that I think that the
real difference between AI

00:20:19.150 --> 00:20:20.920
and traditional
software development

00:20:20.920 --> 00:20:25.510
is that in software development,
you have a set of inputs.

00:20:25.510 --> 00:20:27.760
And you know what
the rules are, right?

00:20:27.760 --> 00:20:29.080
You're coding it up.

00:20:29.080 --> 00:20:32.470
If this, then that, and then
you come up with an output.

00:20:32.470 --> 00:20:34.570
AI is good when you
don't really know what

00:20:34.570 --> 00:20:36.100
those rules should be, right?

00:20:36.100 --> 00:20:39.280
You have a set of inputs, and
you have a set of outputs.

00:20:39.280 --> 00:20:41.410
Like, I have a
bunch of pictures.

00:20:41.410 --> 00:20:44.920
And I know I can label
what's in those pictures.

00:20:44.920 --> 00:20:47.170
But I'm not exactly sure
how I got from point A

00:20:47.170 --> 00:20:49.060
to point B. What were the rules?

00:20:49.060 --> 00:20:52.990
What were the particular pixels
and colors that led to that?

00:20:52.990 --> 00:20:56.110
Or another example
might be you're doing

00:20:56.110 --> 00:20:57.970
some forecasting on the future.

00:20:57.970 --> 00:21:01.150
You're looking at the
markets, and you're

00:21:01.150 --> 00:21:03.520
trying to look at
these trends over time.

00:21:03.520 --> 00:21:05.020
You don't know what
those rules are,

00:21:05.020 --> 00:21:07.810
but that's where machine
learning can come in-- maybe

00:21:07.810 --> 00:21:10.912
that there's some pattern
that can be discovered.

00:21:10.912 --> 00:21:12.370
So essentially what
you're doing is

00:21:12.370 --> 00:21:15.250
you're figuring out
what those rules are,

00:21:15.250 --> 00:21:18.940
versus traditional methods,
you kind of have an approach.

00:21:18.940 --> 00:21:21.970
You know what those
rules or heuristics are.

00:21:21.970 --> 00:21:25.030
So sorry if that was a little
philosophical of an answer,

00:21:25.030 --> 00:21:27.430
but that's kind of
my take on that.

00:21:27.430 --> 00:21:29.590
Zack or Anu, any
other thoughts on when

00:21:29.590 --> 00:21:32.560
to spot a good AI problem?

00:21:32.560 --> 00:21:37.420
ANU SRIVASTAVA: I have I kind of
funny, I guess, rule of thumb.

00:21:37.420 --> 00:21:40.510
You know, if you can
do it with a SQL query,

00:21:40.510 --> 00:21:42.340
and you don't actually
have unknowns,

00:21:42.340 --> 00:21:43.900
it might not be
the right problem.

00:21:43.900 --> 00:21:46.280
But there could be other
problems in your space

00:21:46.280 --> 00:21:48.280
that you need to think
about that don't actually

00:21:48.280 --> 00:21:50.510
have common path that
AI could help you.

00:21:50.510 --> 00:21:54.970
But definitely be careful
because there is a little bit

00:21:54.970 --> 00:21:56.590
of unknown in the mix.

00:21:56.590 --> 00:21:59.080
Check out explainable
AI, because if you're

00:21:59.080 --> 00:22:01.570
doing something
that you have users,

00:22:01.570 --> 00:22:04.630
you want to be really careful
and transparent about what

00:22:04.630 --> 00:22:05.860
you're doing with data.

00:22:05.860 --> 00:22:09.520
So that's my rule
of thumb, I guess.

00:22:09.520 --> 00:22:12.340
ZACK AKIL: I think I've got a
similar thing where it's like,

00:22:12.340 --> 00:22:16.330
rather than knowing what can
be AI, knowing for sure what

00:22:16.330 --> 00:22:19.630
doesn't need to be AI
is as useful of a thing.

00:22:19.630 --> 00:22:22.600
Like, I think there's as a
classic story of how Google

00:22:22.600 --> 00:22:24.430
detects when you
should have attached

00:22:24.430 --> 00:22:26.350
an attachment in a Gmail thing.

00:22:26.350 --> 00:22:28.250
People are like, oh
surely, it's they

00:22:28.250 --> 00:22:31.180
use AI for that to do natural
language classification.

00:22:31.180 --> 00:22:34.010
Like, did they say they
would attach an attachment?

00:22:34.010 --> 00:22:36.880
And if it comes back predicting
they should have, then

00:22:36.880 --> 00:22:39.522
send them a message,
when actually, then, they

00:22:39.522 --> 00:22:41.230
worked as actually
just an if statement--

00:22:41.230 --> 00:22:43.313
if they mention the word
"attachment," "attached,"

00:22:43.313 --> 00:22:44.200
people just use that.

00:22:44.200 --> 00:22:46.367
It's like, if you can solve
it with an if statement,

00:22:46.367 --> 00:22:47.860
probably don't need to use AI.

00:22:47.860 --> 00:22:50.773
But then also a
thing that I think

00:22:50.773 --> 00:22:52.690
will make things a bit
simpler is that there's

00:22:52.690 --> 00:22:54.340
just so many common patterns.

00:22:54.340 --> 00:22:57.040
Like, if you're working
with images, that's classic.

00:22:57.040 --> 00:22:58.240
Video, classic.

00:22:58.240 --> 00:23:01.420
Large bodies of text,
classic AI-- all this kind

00:23:01.420 --> 00:23:03.670
of unstructured data
that doesn't necessarily

00:23:03.670 --> 00:23:05.510
fit into columns
in a database, then

00:23:05.510 --> 00:23:07.760
you can imagine there's
something to do with AI there.

00:23:07.760 --> 00:23:11.290
And what I usually recommend to
people who ask me is, like, oh,

00:23:11.290 --> 00:23:13.636
how would I solve this
specific problem with AI?

00:23:13.636 --> 00:23:16.825
I just ask them, oh, well,
what problem that you know

00:23:16.825 --> 00:23:19.790
